# Routh I.Arts.159-160 — the parabola of safety, and the two ways to hit a
# target — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 159: "To project a particle from a given point P with a given
# velocity V so that it shall pass through another given point Q."
#
# ROUTH'S CONSTRUCTION. The speed at P fixes the COMMON DIRECTRIX of every
# trajectory of that speed, because v^2 = 2g*(depth below the directrix). All
# the parabolas from P with speed V therefore share one horizontal directrix,
# at height V^2/(2g) above P, and their foci all lie on a circle of radius
# V^2/(2g) about P. Requiring the path to pass through Q puts the focus on a
# second circle, about Q. Two circles meet in TWO points -- so a reachable
# target admits exactly TWO trajectories, a flat one and a lofted one -- or
# touch, at the limit of range, or miss entirely.
#
# THE ENVELOPE. The boundary of the reachable set is the PARABOLA OF SAFETY,
#
#     y = V^2/(2g) - g*x^2/(2*V^2)
#
# Inside it, two trajectories; on it, one; outside it, none -- you are safe.
#
# THE ALGEBRA FOR THE TWO ANGLES. Writing t = tan(alpha), a projectile through
# (x, y) satisfies y = x*t - (g*x^2/(2V^2))(1 + t^2), i.e.
#
#     (g x^2 / 2V^2) t^2  -  x t  +  ( y + g x^2 / 2V^2 )  =  0
#
# a quadratic in t. Its discriminant vanishing IS the parabola of safety.
#
# THE NUMBERS. V = 10, g = 9.81, launching from the origin:
#
#     apex of the envelope  V^2/(2g) = 5.09683995922528
#     maximum range         V^2/g    = 10.19367991845056
#     envelope height at x = 6        = 3.33103995922528
#
# Target Q = (6, 2), comfortably inside. The quadratic gives
#
#     high arc: alpha = 68.71729429454481 deg, v = (3.62969990030487, 9.318008297577697, 0)
#               reaching x = 6 at t = 1.6530292213678714
#     low arc:  alpha = 39.71765452837721 deg, v = (7.692026952223115, 6.390048620024201, 0)
#               reaching x = 6 at t = 0.7800284680835533
#
# Both are fired below, each flown to its own arrival time, and both are found
# at (6, 2). Five more projectiles at 20, 35, 50, 65 and 80 degrees are then
# flown together, and for each the clearance
#
#     5.09683995922528 - 0.04905*x^2 - y
#
# is printed: it is non-negative for every one of them at every instant, which
# is the envelope doing its job. (0.04905 = g/(2V^2).)
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0 so the projectiles do not attract one
# another; the uniform field is the only force, which is Routh's problem. With
# no pairwise term they may also share a launch point with no singularity.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a fan of trajectories from one point at one speed, every one
# of them staying under the same invisible parabola, and two of them crossing
# at the same target.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_parabola_of_safety
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_parabola_of_safety.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_parabola_of_safety.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- the two arcs to Q = (6, 2), each flown alone to its own arrival time
set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [3.62969990030487, 9.318008297577697, 0] }
run 1.6530292213678714 steps 2
obj0.position
norm(obj0.position - [6, 2, 0])
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [7.692026952223115, 6.390048620024201, 0] }
run 0.7800284680835533 steps 2
obj0.position
norm(obj0.position - [6, 2, 0])
reset

# ---- a fan of five, all at speed 10: none escapes the envelope
set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [9.396926207859084, 3.420201433256687, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [8.191520442889918, 5.735764363510462, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [6.427876096865394, 7.660444431189780, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [4.226182617406994, 9.063077870366499, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [1.736481776669303, 9.848077530122080, 0] }

run 0.8 steps 1
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj0.position.x * obj0.position.x - obj0.position.y
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj2.position.x * obj2.position.x - obj2.position.y
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj4.position.x * obj4.position.x - obj4.position.y
run 0.7 steps 1
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj0.position.x * obj0.position.x - obj0.position.y
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj2.position.x * obj2.position.x - obj2.position.y
5.09683995922528 - 0.04905 * obj4.position.x * obj4.position.x - obj4.position.y
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.gravity = [0, -9.81, 0]
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [3.62969990030487, 9.318008297577697, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [7.692026952223115, 6.390048620024201, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [9.396926207859084, 3.420201433256687, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [6.427876096865394, 7.660444431189780, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0,0,0], velocity = [1.736481776669303, 9.848077530122080, 0] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
